1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
1890, a magical time.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:09,000
The reason, from this date on, motion can be captured on celluloid.

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:15,000
Sports, disappearing cultures, provocative entertainment, and there's money to be made.

4
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:17,000
Lots of it.

5
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:22,000
Thomas Edison holds many of the patents, but did he really invent motion pictures?

6
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:27,000
Now the real story behind the birth of the movies on modern marvels.

7
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:29,000
ROBERT STACKLIN,

8
00:00:52,526 --> 00:00:54,000
1928, Thomas Elva Edison,

9
00:00:54,001 --> 00:00:57,000
receives the Congressional Gold Medal, the nation's high,

10
00:00:57,001 --> 00:00:58,960
highest civilian honor.

11
00:00:58,961 --> 00:01:02,500
At 81, he's a living legend.

12
00:01:02,501 --> 00:01:05,500
The man who'd laid the technological foundations

13
00:01:05,501 --> 00:01:08,520
for the 20th century and created motion pictures

14
00:01:08,521 --> 00:01:11,520
to record this new world for posterity,

15
00:01:11,521 --> 00:01:15,980
along with his own accomplishments.

16
00:01:15,981 --> 00:01:19,660
The first motion picture studio ever set up in the world

17
00:01:19,661 --> 00:01:22,960
was in the yard of my laboratory at West Orange.

18
00:01:22,961 --> 00:01:25,720
The building revolved to follow the sun.

19
00:01:25,721 --> 00:01:26,900
We dubbed it the black blue.

20
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:32,000
As recreated with his own invention, the motion picture,

21
00:01:32,001 --> 00:01:36,000
Edison was portrayed as a mix of Horatio Alger Pluck,

22
00:01:36,001 --> 00:01:40,000
homespun Yankee ingenuity, and visionary genius.

23
00:01:40,001 --> 00:01:46,000
These are scenes from a 1915 epic entitled The Benefactor.

24
00:01:46,001 --> 00:01:51,000
From the beginning, his life had the makings of a great movie.

25
00:01:51,001 --> 00:01:57,000
In a sense, Edison devised his own legend of this sort of

26
00:01:57,001 --> 00:02:01,040
rough-hewn inventor who didn't pay much attention to science.

27
00:02:01,280 --> 00:02:03,140
In fact, Edison paid a lot of attention to science.

28
00:02:03,240 --> 00:02:08,600
What he didn't pay attention to was the kind of higher-level pure science that didn't

29
00:02:08,601 --> 00:02:10,420
have a media practical application.

30
00:02:11,260 --> 00:02:16,740
Thirty years old, Edison applied his practical genius and produced the talking machine.

31
00:02:17,800 --> 00:02:22,600
Topping himself two years later, he lit up the world with the electric light.

32
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:26,980
By then, their...

33
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:30,480
There were people who believed he could do anything.

34
00:02:30,481 --> 00:02:34,420
Most of all, Edison himself.

35
00:02:34,421 --> 00:02:37,320
Most inventors have rather large egos.

36
00:02:37,321 --> 00:02:42,120
They feel they can affect the way other people live their lives, and the way they can do

37
00:02:42,121 --> 00:02:48,160
that is not through management or necessarily through ideas, intellectual ideas, but rather

38
00:02:48,161 --> 00:02:54,580
through creating technologies and systems that people will live with and work with.

39
00:02:54,581 --> 00:02:56,980
Edison's self-absorbed involvement with his work.

40
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:00,800
had disastrous effects on a first marriage.

41
00:03:00,801 --> 00:03:04,040
Weakened by declining health, his wife Mary Stillwell

42
00:03:04,041 --> 00:03:11,440
died in 1884, neglected, and mentally unstable.

43
00:03:11,441 --> 00:03:17,360
Left with three children, Edison remarried in 1887.

44
00:03:17,361 --> 00:03:23,160
His second wife, Mina Miller, was the daughter of a millionaire.

45
00:03:23,161 --> 00:03:27,000
Edison remained Edison, but with the support of family and friends,

46
00:03:27,001 --> 00:03:30,500
Mena was better prepared to be the wife of a man called

47
00:03:30,501 --> 00:03:33,000
the Wizard of Menlo Park.

48
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:37,000
Bursting with ideas for new inventions,

49
00:03:37,001 --> 00:03:42,000
in 1887, Edison moved into a huge new laboratory complex

50
00:03:42,001 --> 00:03:44,000
in West Orange, New Jersey.

51
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:47,000
Ten times larger than his old quarters

52
00:03:47,001 --> 00:03:50,000
a few miles away in the village of Menlo Park.

53
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:54,000
For Maina and his children, he bought Glenmont,

54
00:03:54,001 --> 00:03:56,000
a lavish nearby estate.

55
00:03:56,001 --> 00:03:56,701
estate.

56
00:03:56,702 --> 00:04:04,020
Settled in West Orange, it wasn't long before Edison found himself at his desk in his

57
00:04:04,021 --> 00:04:12,701
library thinking about the realization of a very old dream, moving pictures.

58
00:04:12,702 --> 00:04:15,100
This book, written in

59
00:04:15,101 --> 00:04:23,100
1646 by Affamacious Kircher, a Jesuit priest, records the early history of projected images.

60
00:04:23,101 --> 00:04:28,260
It began with the magic lantern, forerunner of today's slide projector.

61
00:04:30,260 --> 00:04:37,340
The magic lantern was a device that used light from a lamp to project images painted on glass plates.

62
00:04:38,640 --> 00:04:44,300
The effect on viewers was truly magical, and these were only static drawings.

63
00:04:45,880 --> 00:04:52,140
Eager for the energy of motion, early inventors created ingenious devices to add life to their shows.

64
00:04:53,100 --> 00:04:57,100
Slides with movable parts were popular.

65
00:04:57,101 --> 00:05:02,100
A more sophisticated illusion of movement came in 1834

66
00:05:02,101 --> 00:05:08,100
with the creation of the zoetrope by English inventor George Homer.

67
00:05:08,101 --> 00:05:13,100
Here, spinning, sequential images appear to move.

68
00:05:13,101 --> 00:05:18,100
It was an example of a physiological phenomenon described in 1884

69
00:05:18,101 --> 00:05:22,100
by Peter Mark Roge of thesaurus fame.

70
00:05:22,101 --> 00:05:27,100
It's called persistence of vision.

71
00:05:27,101 --> 00:05:30,100
With cinema, when you project an image on the screen,

72
00:05:30,101 --> 00:05:32,100
which creates the illusion of motion,

73
00:05:32,101 --> 00:05:36,100
what you're projecting on the screen is really a series of still photographs.

74
00:05:36,101 --> 00:05:39,100
And what happens is that the eye synthesized

75
00:05:39,101 --> 00:05:42,100
these slightly different photographs in such a way

76
00:05:42,101 --> 00:05:45,100
that it creates the illusion of motion.

77
00:05:45,101 --> 00:05:50,100
A popular early toy illustrates this synthesis or blending by the brain.

78
00:05:50,101 --> 00:05:54,100
When a card with a picture of a bird on one side,

79
00:05:54,101 --> 00:05:57,100
in a picture of a bird cage on the other is twirled rapidly,

80
00:05:57,101 --> 00:05:59,100
the brain combines the two pictures,

81
00:05:59,101 --> 00:06:03,100
creating the illusion that the bird is in the cage.

82
00:06:03,101 --> 00:06:09,100
The zoetrope and many devices like it

83
00:06:09,101 --> 00:06:13,100
became popular in 19th century parlor amusements.

84
00:06:13,101 --> 00:06:17,100
But in 1829, a vital step in the evolution

85
00:06:17,101 --> 00:06:19,100
of the motion picture took place.

86
00:06:19,101 --> 00:06:27,100
Louis Degere perfected a truly magical technology, photography.

87
00:06:27,101 --> 00:06:35,100
Now, for the first time, a glimpse of the real world had been captured forever, frozen on a light-sensitive glass plate.

88
00:06:35,101 --> 00:06:44,100
But creating early photographs took as long as five minutes per image, too slow for movies.

89
00:06:44,101 --> 00:06:48,100
By the 1870s, faster picture-taking was possible.

90
00:06:48,101 --> 00:06:48,824
possible.

91
00:06:48,825 --> 00:06:55,100
This allowed Edward Moybridge studying the movement of animals to make another

92
00:06:55,101 --> 00:07:03,100
major step toward motion pictures. California railroad magnate Leland Stanford hired

93
00:07:03,101 --> 00:07:05,159
Moibridge to prove a bet.

94
00:07:05,160 --> 00:07:10,100
Did all four hooves of a running horse ever leave the ground

95
00:07:10,101 --> 00:07:18,080
at the same time? Working at Stanford's Palo Alto farm, Moybridge lined up a

96
00:07:18,100 --> 00:07:23,040
a series of cameras along a short track.

97
00:07:23,041 --> 00:07:25,340
Threads stretched across the track were attached

98
00:07:25,341 --> 00:07:29,460
to each of the camera shutters.

99
00:07:29,461 --> 00:07:31,820
When a horse raced by it broke the threads,

100
00:07:31,821 --> 00:07:37,520
which in turn released the camera shutters one after another.

101
00:07:37,521 --> 00:07:43,300
The result was a series of sequential photographs.

102
00:07:43,301 --> 00:07:47,160
Sure enough, there was a time when all four of the horse's

103
00:07:47,161 --> 00:07:50,540
His legs were off the ground, but more important,

104
00:07:50,541 --> 00:07:54,000
Moibridge painted copies of his photographs on a glass wheel

105
00:07:54,001 --> 00:07:58,200
and created a viewing device called the Zupraxoscope.

106
00:07:58,201 --> 00:08:00,500
For the first time, there was a photographically

107
00:08:00,501 --> 00:08:03,400
accurate illusion of movement.

108
00:08:03,401 --> 00:08:06,120
When Thomas Edison heard of Moibridge's work,

109
00:08:06,121 --> 00:08:09,240
he was intrigued.

110
00:08:09,241 --> 00:08:13,280
In 1888, he and Moibridge meant.

111
00:08:13,281 --> 00:08:15,280
Who knows what they talked about entirely,

112
00:08:15,281 --> 00:08:17,120
but certainly one thing they did talk about

113
00:08:17,160 --> 00:08:21,660
how to possibly link the zoopraxoscope with Edison's phonograph.

114
00:08:22,300 --> 00:08:25,480
Edison had sort of found, to his surprise,

115
00:08:25,640 --> 00:08:28,140
that some of his competitors had made major innovations

116
00:08:28,141 --> 00:08:30,120
on his tinfoil phonograph,

117
00:08:30,460 --> 00:08:32,380
and he was trying to one-up them.

118
00:08:33,520 --> 00:08:35,800
Photographs that moved and talked,

119
00:08:36,040 --> 00:08:37,860
now that would shake up the competition.

120
00:08:38,700 --> 00:08:41,240
But work on movies didn't begin immediately.

121
00:08:42,340 --> 00:08:44,460
Edison's restless mind was elsewhere.

122
00:08:45,700 --> 00:08:47,140
At a mine, he owned in no.

123
00:08:47,160 --> 00:08:52,720
Nearby Ogdensburg, he was focused on a little known obsession of his life, a machine to

124
00:08:52,721 --> 00:08:55,640
separate iron ore with large magnets.

125
00:08:57,400 --> 00:09:02,060
It was typical of Edison to work on many ideas at the same time.

126
00:09:03,560 --> 00:09:07,340
In 1888, moving pictures was added to his list.

127
00:09:08,440 --> 00:09:14,880
To help, he chose a trusted assistant from the ore mining project, William Kennedy, Lori Dixon,

128
00:09:14,881 --> 00:09:19,440
an avid photographer who took most of the pictures around the lab,

129
00:09:20,020 --> 00:09:24,900
including this famous portrait of Edison as the Napoleon of Invention.

130
00:09:27,480 --> 00:09:30,560
The motion picture project was perfect for Dixon.

131
00:09:31,220 --> 00:09:36,720
From the beginning, Edison conceived of a motion picture machine linked to the phonograph,

132
00:09:37,420 --> 00:09:39,000
sound and pictures together.

133
00:09:40,360 --> 00:09:44,440
On October 8, 1888, he wrote a caveat, or purlidate,

134
00:09:44,441 --> 00:09:48,440
preliminary announcement of his work in progress.

135
00:09:48,441 --> 00:09:55,440
I am experimenting upon an instrument which does for the eye what the phonograph does for the ear,

136
00:09:55,441 --> 00:10:02,440
which is the recording and reproduction of things in motion.

137
00:10:02,441 --> 00:10:09,440
The new invention was called the kinetoscope from the Greek words for motion and view.

138
00:10:09,441 --> 00:10:14,420
Dixon's job was to turn this exotic name into a working

139
00:10:14,440 --> 00:10:15,140
machine.

140
00:10:17,340 --> 00:10:24,420
With time, his role changed from assistant to collaborator, to some say, unaccredited co-inventor.

141
00:10:26,420 --> 00:10:33,280
Edison literally was not present in West Orange, five or six days of the week.

142
00:10:33,380 --> 00:10:38,820
He was up in northwestern Jersey on his iron ore mine.

143
00:10:39,720 --> 00:10:44,420
Dixon was the proprietor of the lab, and he was the man who was working.

144
00:10:44,440 --> 00:10:47,180
most closely on developing the technology

145
00:10:47,181 --> 00:10:48,980
for the motion picture.

146
00:10:48,981 --> 00:10:53,180
Edison's first idea was to wrap a piece of film

147
00:10:53,181 --> 00:10:58,280
on a cylinder and expose a spiral of tiny photographs.

148
00:10:58,281 --> 00:11:00,280
But to fit on a phonograph cylinder,

149
00:11:00,281 --> 00:11:05,200
Dixon needed to make the pictures extremely small.

150
00:11:05,201 --> 00:11:08,200
Produced sometime in 1889, these are frames

151
00:11:08,201 --> 00:11:11,100
from the earliest surviving motion picture,

152
00:11:11,101 --> 00:11:13,300
nicknamed Monkey Shines.

153
00:11:13,301 --> 00:11:18,300
To see them, a viewer had to look through a microscope.

154
00:11:18,301 --> 00:11:23,300
Here, the individual frames have been reconstructed into movement.

155
00:11:23,301 --> 00:11:29,300
An agile pipe fitter from the island of Malta was drafted to star.

156
00:11:29,301 --> 00:11:36,300
Lasting only a few seconds, the image was crude and the sound now lost,

157
00:11:36,301 --> 00:11:38,300
unintelligible.

158
00:11:38,301 --> 00:11:42,300
Still by the end of eight years,

159
00:11:42,301 --> 00:11:49,180
By the end of 1890 after centuries of searching false starts and dead ends, the dream

160
00:11:49,181 --> 00:11:53,180
of moving pictures was about to be realized.

161
00:11:53,181 --> 00:12:01,060
But Edison and Dixon weren't the only men nearing the solution.

162
00:12:01,061 --> 00:12:05,180
Edison is said to have first become inspired to experiment with motion pictures when he saw

163
00:12:05,181 --> 00:12:09,340
Edward Moybridge's motion study of a woman adjusting her dress.

164
00:12:12,300 --> 00:12:22,780
When Thomas Edison began experimenting with motion pictures in 1888, he wasn't alone.

165
00:12:23,500 --> 00:12:28,220
In the later part of the 19th century, there were a whole series of technological innovations

166
00:12:28,221 --> 00:12:32,660
that really made the cinema, made modern motion pictures possible.

167
00:12:33,260 --> 00:12:38,760
And there were an array of people in Europe, in France, in England, in Germany, in the United States,

168
00:12:38,761 --> 00:12:42,280
who were experimenting with these elements and trying to pull them together.

169
00:12:42,300 --> 00:12:45,300
in things that resembled motion pictures.

170
00:12:45,301 --> 00:12:49,300
In France, there was Etienne Jean-Marais.

171
00:12:49,301 --> 00:12:53,300
Like Edison, Moray was influenced by Edward Moybridge's work.

172
00:12:53,301 --> 00:12:59,300
He too was interested in studying the movements of animals with photography.

173
00:12:59,301 --> 00:13:05,300
But unlike Moybridge's multi-camera setup, he developed a single-camera system.

174
00:13:05,301 --> 00:13:12,280
Morey's camera looked like a rifle, but the cylinders of his

175
00:13:12,300 --> 00:13:18,300
photographic gun contained lenses, not bullets.

176
00:13:18,301 --> 00:13:21,300
When he pulled the trigger, the cylinder revolved,

177
00:13:21,301 --> 00:13:26,300
taking sequential photographs on a strip of film.

178
00:13:26,301 --> 00:13:32,300
In 1889, with his cylinder motion picture work approaching a dead end,

179
00:13:32,301 --> 00:13:35,300
Edison decided to go to Paris for an exhibition

180
00:13:35,301 --> 00:13:40,300
marking the 50th anniversary of the invention of photography.

181
00:13:40,301 --> 00:13:44,300
At the exhibition Edison's display of electrical marvels

182
00:13:44,301 --> 00:13:46,300
was a Parisian triumph,

183
00:13:46,301 --> 00:13:49,300
as memorable as the new Eiffel Tower.

184
00:13:51,300 --> 00:13:55,300
Motion pictures were probably far from his mind.

185
00:13:57,300 --> 00:13:59,300
Despite this, unknowingly,

186
00:13:59,301 --> 00:14:02,300
Edison was also approaching a turning point in his work

187
00:14:02,301 --> 00:14:05,300
toward true moving pictures.

188
00:14:05,301 --> 00:14:09,300
In Paris, he met many scientists and researchers,

189
00:14:09,301 --> 00:14:14,920
Etienne Jean-Marais, the inventor of the photographic gun, was one of them.

190
00:14:15,840 --> 00:14:23,100
It's very important to remember this in terms of understanding Edison's successful inventive process.

191
00:14:23,400 --> 00:14:30,480
He did not get these ideas simply out of some corner recess in his brain.

192
00:14:30,860 --> 00:14:32,160
He read widely.

193
00:14:32,800 --> 00:14:37,940
He attempted to find out what everybody else was doing in order to incorporate these ideas

194
00:14:37,941 --> 00:14:40,680
and give them a new twist.

195
00:14:41,780 --> 00:14:44,900
Back in America, in October 1889,

196
00:14:45,400 --> 00:14:48,700
Edison changed the direction of his motion picture research.

197
00:14:49,640 --> 00:14:51,620
He abandoned the cylinder concept.

198
00:14:52,800 --> 00:14:55,420
Instead, he wanted perforated strips of film,

199
00:14:55,920 --> 00:14:57,860
a variation of Moray's idea.

200
00:15:00,260 --> 00:15:03,740
Edison's assistant, W.K.L. Dixon was soon at work

201
00:15:03,741 --> 00:15:05,820
on a new motion picture machine.

202
00:15:05,821 --> 00:15:13,220
Unlike today's cameras and projectors, the film moved laterally through the mechanism, not top to bottom.

203
00:15:14,180 --> 00:15:18,640
In March of 1890, Dixon was ready to show his preliminary handiwork.

204
00:15:19,420 --> 00:15:20,980
Edison was impressed.

205
00:15:22,260 --> 00:15:23,220
That's it, he said.

206
00:15:23,460 --> 00:15:24,340
Work like hell.

207
00:15:25,020 --> 00:15:25,800
Dixon did.

208
00:15:26,720 --> 00:15:29,300
Working with his partner machinist Thomas Heiss.

209
00:15:29,301 --> 00:15:34,740
The two are seen on a frame from an 1892 experimental film.

210
00:15:35,820 --> 00:15:39,820
Together, they were inventing motion pictures.

211
00:15:39,821 --> 00:15:45,820
They started with film strips three-quarter inches wide,

212
00:15:45,821 --> 00:15:47,820
moving later to one and a half-inch strips,

213
00:15:47,821 --> 00:15:51,820
close to today's standard of 35 millimeter.

214
00:15:51,821 --> 00:15:55,820
Sprocket holes were punched with a manual perforator.

215
00:15:55,821 --> 00:16:02,820
On May 20th, 1891, Dixon had made enough progress

216
00:16:02,821 --> 00:16:04,820
for a surprise premiere.

217
00:16:04,821 --> 00:16:12,360
On that day, a group of women were visiting Edison's wife, Mina, at Glenmont.

218
00:16:13,200 --> 00:16:18,340
They were ushered into the lab where one by one they were asked to look into a wooden box.

219
00:16:20,800 --> 00:16:26,200
Inside, they saw something startling, a photograph of Dixon that moved.

220
00:16:28,040 --> 00:16:32,820
The little black and white image gallantly removed his straw hat.

221
00:16:32,821 --> 00:16:37,780
These are the few remaining frames of that landmark movie.

222
00:16:39,260 --> 00:16:45,520
Minus Luncheon guests were the first outsiders to see the still experimental kinetoscope in action,

223
00:16:46,180 --> 00:16:50,240
and Edison's little surprise wasn't a casual decision.

224
00:16:50,880 --> 00:16:55,680
Edison was aware that he was working against a whole group of would-be competitors,

225
00:16:55,920 --> 00:17:01,700
and so I think as soon as an experiment was perfected or even able to be shown,

226
00:17:01,701 --> 00:17:05,880
he would find an opportunity or an excuse to show it to a public

227
00:17:05,881 --> 00:17:08,720
because that became key evidence in patent battles.

228
00:17:09,640 --> 00:17:12,320
But he would always maintain that it had been around for a long time

229
00:17:12,321 --> 00:17:15,000
so that if someone came up with an earlier date,

230
00:17:15,300 --> 00:17:16,480
he could fudge it a little bit.

231
00:17:16,520 --> 00:17:19,040
And he, in fact, was the master of fudging dates.

232
00:17:20,060 --> 00:17:22,720
Despite its private debut for Minus Friends,

233
00:17:22,900 --> 00:17:24,620
the kinetoscope still needed work.

234
00:17:26,080 --> 00:17:29,920
The images were blurry and the action jerky.

235
00:17:29,921 --> 00:17:33,580
But instead of rushing to improve his invention,

236
00:17:34,080 --> 00:17:36,100
Edison turned again to other work.

237
00:17:37,100 --> 00:17:41,260
It seemed like he was always juggling too many projects.

238
00:17:43,460 --> 00:17:46,820
This notebook contains strips of experimental film,

239
00:17:47,420 --> 00:17:50,820
side by side with notes for Edison's ore mining project.

240
00:17:51,900 --> 00:17:55,160
It wasn't until a year later, July 1891,

241
00:17:55,900 --> 00:17:59,040
that he applied for the kinetoscope's first basic patent.

242
00:17:59,920 --> 00:18:03,920
Only two years later in 1893,

243
00:18:03,921 --> 00:18:05,920
Edison's fortunes were down.

244
00:18:05,921 --> 00:18:09,920
He was in need of another money-making Marvel.

245
00:18:09,921 --> 00:18:13,920
He decided to announce the kinetoscope

